# 12. Functions 5 and 6: Exporting ROS interfaces

These are among the most important functions to understand.

## `export_state_interfaces()`

Your code includes:

```cpp
interfaces.emplace_back(
    "base_left_wheel_joint",
    hardware_interface::HW_IF_POSITION,
    &hw_position_[kLeft]);
```

This creates a hardware state interface.

Let's examine its three arguments.

| Argument | Meaning |
|---|---|
| `"base_left_wheel_joint"` | Joint name |
| `HW_IF_POSITION` | Type of state |
| `&hw_position_[kLeft]` | Address of the variable holding the measured position |

### Understanding the `&` operator

Here:

```cpp
&hw_position_[kLeft]
```

means:

> Give the memory address of the left wheel's position variable.

For example, imagine:

```cpp
hw_position_[0] = 1.57;
```

This variable is stored somewhere in memory.

The state interface holds a pointer referring to it.

When your hardware code updates the value:

```cpp
hw_position_[0] = 2.15;
```

ROS 2 can access the updated value through the exported interface.

You do not need to manually publish it as a ROS topic inside this class.

This function exposes four state interfaces:

```text
base_left_wheel_joint/position

base_left_wheel_joint/velocity

base_right_wheel_joint/position

base_right_wheel_joint/velocity
```

The code uses `emplace_back()` to construct each interface directly in the vector.

## `export_command_interfaces()`

Now look at:

```cpp
interfaces.emplace_back(
    "base_left_wheel_joint",
    hardware_interface::HW_IF_VELOCITY,
    &hw_command_[kLeft]);
```

This exposes a writable velocity command interface.

The controller can provide a target wheel velocity through this interface.

The function exposes:

```text
base_left_wheel_joint/velocity

base_right_wheel_joint/velocity
```

### The fundamental difference

```text
         COMMAND INTERFACE
                 |
                 ▼
          hw_command_[0]
                 |
                 ▼
           write()
                 |
                 ▼
              Motor


               Encoder
                 |
                 ▼
              read()
                 |
                 ▼
           hw_position_[0]
           hw_velocity_[0]
                 |
                 ▼
           STATE INTERFACE
```

In short:

**Command interfaces tell the hardware what to do. State interfaces tell ROS 2 what the hardware actually did.**

This separation is fundamental to `ros2_control`.

