# 14. Function 8: `send_payload()` — Constructing the serial frame

This function adds a checksum and transmits the message.

Your code:

```cpp
frame << payload
      << '*'
      << std::uppercase
      << std::hex
      << std::setw(4)
      << std::setfill('0')
      << crc16_ccitt(payload)
      << '\n';
```

Let's understand the structure.

Suppose `write()` generates:

```text
C,1,1500,1500
```

The checksum calculation for this exact payload gives:

```text
9FCB
```

Therefore, the transmitted frame becomes:

```text
C,1,1500,1500*9FCB\n
```

Here:

| Element | Meaning |
|---|---|
| `C` | Command message |
| `1` | Sequence number |
| `1500` | Left wheel target velocity |
| `1500` | Right wheel target velocity |
| `*` | Payload/checksum separator |
| `9FCB` | CRC checksum |
| `\n` | End of message |

The code uses:

```cpp
::write(serial_fd_, ...)
```

to transmit the bytes through Linux's serial device interface.

Notice that this is different from:

```cpp
DiffBotSystem::write()
```

The leading `::` selects the global POSIX `write()` function rather than your class's `write()` method.

The implementation also handles partially completed writes by continuing until all bytes have been sent, or returning an error. Pasted text(20261112-223007)

