# 17. Function 11: `crc16_ccitt()`

```cpp
static uint16_t crc16_ccitt(const std::string & text);
```

This function calculates a **16-bit cyclic redundancy check**.

A CRC helps detect accidental changes to data during communication.

Suppose the original command is:

```text
C,1,1500,1500
```

But due to corruption, the received text becomes:

```text
C,1,1500,9500
```

The calculated CRC will generally differ from the transmitted CRC.

The receiver can therefore reject the corrupted frame.

Your implementation uses:

```cpp
uint16_t crc = 0xFFFF;
```

with the polynomial:

```cpp
0x1021
```

It processes every byte and then every bit of the payload.

This is a CRC-16/CCITT-FALSE-style calculation.

For reliable communication, the Arduino must implement a compatible checksum algorithm using the same initialization, polynomial, and processing conventions.

### Why is the function `static`?

Here, `static` means the member function belongs to the class rather than to a specific object instance.

It does not require access to the object's member variables.

Therefore, it can calculate a checksum using only its input text.

