# 19. How does ROS 2 find your class?

At the bottom of your `.cpp` file:

```cpp
PLUGINLIB_EXPORT_CLASS(
    my_robot_hardware::DiffBotSystem,
    hardware_interface::SystemInterface)
```

This is a ROS 2 plugin registration macro.

Your code is compiled into a shared library.

ROS 2's plugin infrastructure can then load the hardware interface dynamically.

Conceptually:

```text
          Robot URDF
              |
              ▼
       <ros2_control>
              |
              ▼
         Plugin Name
              |
              ▼
         pluginlib
              |
              ▼
        Shared Library
              |
              ▼
        DiffBotSystem
```

This generally requires the appropriate plugin description XML, CMake configuration, and package metadata as well.

The plugin export line alone is not sufficient to make an entire ROS 2 package loadable.

Your `DiffBotSystem` class does not need its own `main()` function because it is loaded and managed by ROS 2's controller manager.

