# 23. The best way to learn this code

Rather than trying to memorize all 337 lines of the `.cpp` file, I recommend learning it in this order:

| Stage | Topic | Main C++ concepts |
|---|---|---|
| 1 | Header file | Classes, objects, inheritance, namespaces |
| 2 | Member variables | Arrays, strings, initialization, data types |
| 3 | ROS hardware interfaces | Vectors, pointers, memory addresses |
| 4 | Lifecycle functions | Functions, return values, conditional statements |
| 5 | `write()` | Data conversion, string streams, arithmetic |
| 6 | `read()` | Buffers, loops, string manipulation |
| 7 | Serial communication | Linux system calls, file descriptors |
| 8 | CRC | Bitwise operations, hexadecimal numbers |
| 9 | Full ROS integration | Plugins, controllers, hardware lifecycle |

### The five functions you should understand first

For your physical DiffBot, I would prioritize:

**`on_init()` → `export_command_interfaces()` → `write()` → `read()` → `process_line()`**

These functions explain most of the connection between your ROS 2 controller and the physical wheels.

Once those are clear, the remaining functions become considerably easier.

